#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "ac2a2137-53fa-5121-b541-02e0addd8757" :question/math-academy-id "e-12356" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](images/41/4152d997be216db1a67b5f55ee112d1008ea7399e57f699ac62ba03654bd7fd5.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{(z)^{2}+(-y)^{2}+(-x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz-2yz-x(z-y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "fba5ed01-4c4c-50f6-8060-76bf40df79b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12384" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "35c3c9ba-60b0-5bcf-8e9c-2862ece44e7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12454" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||\\mathbf r_x \\times \\mathbf r_y|| =3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,0,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2y+0x+2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83738cc5-1219-53e4-93d6-345faec69645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163847} {:answer/id #uuid "a12a7922-8eb4-5d92-9e3e-1a8d7b5d62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163848} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b3e9e5-8abf-5dec-a35f-5de044772de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163849} {:answer/id #uuid "db6f1f26-9351-585d-a665-5e9fa2429532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163850} {:answer/id #uuid "34f7149c-476d-5102-abff-6823eff76396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a12a7922-8eb4-5d92-9e3e-1a8d7b5d62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d81c30-07ea-532f-87c1-60b50a92525b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dea0b9b2-aa9a-54da-ab21-772ddcf1f781" :question/math-academy-id "q-161152" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e65dddf-58c2-5f17-8235-bfb8e2243bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421187} {:answer/id #uuid "5df024a4-000b-5ecc-b480-68e2ad920b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421191} {:answer/id #uuid "82ef2bb9-743f-5b02-a915-dfb52e0d086a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421202} {:answer/id #uuid "958fb760-98a9-540e-9910-e0ef71c1de34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421205} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9e92e2-eb3e-592a-a5c1-dd41305c03d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958fb760-98a9-540e-9910-e0ef71c1de34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421205} :answer-field/id #uuid "2593ab3d-fc5b-5105-99bf-0103c2b5420d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c6d18b-d618-53cd-9fce-a599b57a0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161186" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118324c2-6877-51fd-9281-af0fdd0a2878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421177} {:answer/id #uuid "31bdabd7-159e-575d-bb25-4c387ed44025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421182} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7efde4-f50f-5cc1-8aa7-c78fcd23996d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421208} {:answer/id #uuid "c52866a7-6394-5695-8e68-74afc198f090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421214} {:answer/id #uuid "d77c0b45-5ae9-59ba-8063-f366351598fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d77c0b45-5ae9-59ba-8063-f366351598fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421218} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf0284d-4596-58d3-b54a-194c38549624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6480541e-888b-58cb-a4eb-1a531ae87114" :question/math-academy-id "q-161197" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a  continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4},$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1/4}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de3a55c-a3c2-5a9a-a812-bcdfecfb6feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421180} {:answer/id #uuid "60427d20-92ba-5d11-ad42-138a2d763370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421193} {:answer/id #uuid "69182493-5072-5c9f-a5f1-7e563c775c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y+\\frac{z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421194} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ece12f-0d28-5947-8017-589e0f4fd627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421213} {:answer/id #uuid "f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421222} :answer-field/id #uuid "396f5c74-b8d5-5eee-bb2c-03c6070bb704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-161202" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2z+0-3(z-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y-z}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{3y-z}{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}3y-z\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372a4ff1-3939-5a9d-945d-86bafcb4f341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421185} {:answer/id #uuid "56bb391e-d6f2-5324-8833-628a5044bcbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421190} {:answer/id #uuid "6c99d7a5-4c32-540a-9715-3adff1719111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421195} {:answer/id #uuid "7232adec-c36a-56bf-ab40-f21a586d8c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421197} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa4bbee-939a-5f5a-a82e-c6752d30386c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}-{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7232adec-c36a-56bf-ab40-f21a586d8c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421197} :answer-field/id #uuid "2190c13e-d51f-542e-bd67-2b30e8f5c940" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7df86f0-3809-56f0-adcd-fb286616dfa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161204" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{{({y}^{2})}^{2}+(2x)^{2}+(-z)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\cdot ⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}y-z({y}^{2}-1)}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421176} {:answer/id #uuid "25f30ee3-665e-5e1b-aa22-c26247f952a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421179} {:answer/id #uuid "7bde0ba6-3997-5ac1-85d6-17921e2bfa0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2yz+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421199} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3835b0-1d08-5073-b2ca-5face96ff967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+yz-xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421201} {:answer/id #uuid "8af4099a-a790-588b-a510-ac4dbee0f11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421176} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea0f599-a5c9-5a71-bef4-9b89d6f5c991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b743d271-bfde-5d80-a2b4-b7cbdaa6f1f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161206" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}+(y)^{2}+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+3{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+yz}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{3{x}^{3}+yz}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319237d3-bef2-5a87-9ee7-4240f5045911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421181} {:answer/id #uuid "967a8907-2231-5e99-a474-67c6bc6bd9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421206} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b43aea-006a-5e99-8f2a-f05fd804cb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421215} {:answer/id #uuid "ca025449-86ec-5c0d-a704-da1d0fa9dc55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xy+y+z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421216} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f51faf-c287-515f-9df7-2b6d99509772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1f51faf-c287-515f-9df7-2b6d99509772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421221} :answer-field/id #uuid "e88ac117-843b-5a55-b610-415191bfff7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75809447-c869-5206-aa76-8147e22ce3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161208" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{(xy)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xyz-xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c959028-5450-5d3b-9b24-6daaa18a88b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421209} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e05106-52da-5edd-b6ad-f2bf96a94e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421211} {:answer/id #uuid "ceabd340-e0b0-5970-af89-cfe6906a0509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421217} {:answer/id #uuid "ea827a58-c8d0-530c-aa8a-a0149d25105d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421220} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f0d6e-3bb7-580f-b6e6-18fcc86988ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+2y+3z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea827a58-c8d0-530c-aa8a-a0149d25105d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421220} :answer-field/id #uuid "662e48f4-a2f4-58ca-946c-9c7fd0f8d49d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5213d5bf-5843-5c8c-8790-014b534c60aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161211" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{(-3z)^{2}+(x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{9{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}z+2y(x+y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f614dd-3b95-57de-8bba-fbdf8531494d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421189} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee0e41b-d6ea-5017-8c8a-6f46a0e3dbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421196} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6976ed-177a-5352-84ae-a730d2e02dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421203} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd1bd4e-9eca-533a-bc90-7301652e24d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421212} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd79be2-e35a-5ca5-821c-28d7b26f68a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd1bd4e-9eca-533a-bc90-7301652e24d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421212} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4704df-0892-5011-9881-9914abd2c673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa928e5a-70af-5b00-a980-606761f10bda" :question/math-academy-id "q-161243" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=3,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,(2x-y+6)-x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,x-2y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x-2y-2(x-2y+6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-6y]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a50551bb-2bfa-545f-bfec-d2070d12162f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163859} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6f0a12-f232-5e2b-b2f1-2e7b023264fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163860} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1dbc88-e2c8-53cd-823e-c82d725a89d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163861} {:answer/id #uuid "fd394791-056b-5b7b-b1af-f84d65cc9169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163862} {:answer/id #uuid "0d264df1-7f0b-5421-8eb3-0926a18534a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d264df1-7f0b-5421-8eb3-0926a18534a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163863} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab8ce14-f8c3-5bfc-a6f7-64b0895b0b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07582355-f319-5419-a760-27151ce99dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161244" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y-2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+2y-2$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-2,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=2.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y-2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,y,\\,2x+2y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2y-(2x+2y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd556fd2-277f-5c8e-9b21-8802757469bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163865} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fcef11-1d4a-5fc1-89d3-3c46337f10b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163866} {:answer/id #uuid "db2a56e3-5b85-5f3d-b99f-327b814e5837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163867} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5d670d-a079-52a1-be8c-383c7e35c122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163868} {:answer/id #uuid "602bdb6d-95e4-538b-b0ed-dcf372ecf31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "602bdb6d-95e4-538b-b0ed-dcf372ecf31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163869} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b2ef6c-c409-5bdc-8c17-bfe2e3100c8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d42bef6-ce15-55df-885d-72410b164f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162146" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z+3⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x+y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{3}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+y+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-x-y+(x+y+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33f99146-c5ea-58b5-9bc1-58895cc4fc3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421183} {:answer/id #uuid "351f00b6-bf80-5f84-ad64-40be3bbe2fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421184} {:answer/id #uuid "5e434de3-deea-53ed-838e-cf948a5ae65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421192} {:answer/id #uuid "74c21c89-a987-5d09-9152-140d29002bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421198} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb7492c-b0fb-5b7c-a8dc-51bc27779fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "351f00b6-bf80-5f84-ad64-40be3bbe2fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421184} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f85e1d-9f83-541a-b342-23041c17ed8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49459c91-bfac-5582-aa3d-bc1bfea60a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162157" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+x,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=-x+2y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=2\\sqrt{3}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=-x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=-x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+x,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2y,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2y-2(x+y)+2x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03158da1-b1d2-5e08-9fb3-bcf52d0b7ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421175} {:answer/id #uuid "228c1e6f-6068-550e-b050-12aa6caa4351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421178} {:answer/id #uuid "3cae5a31-54ec-52d7-9829-e6f79d0db7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421186} {:answer/id #uuid "52b6e1b8-f74d-549f-80b2-b5dec0190106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421188} {:answer/id #uuid "974560cd-4e8d-59bd-8077-c163da225937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "974560cd-4e8d-59bd-8077-c163da225937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421207} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f22f763-7742-563a-8020-3b6c5519f8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f0f5aff-a329-55d0-a382-ebd7a7992774" :question/math-academy-id "q-162548" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-4y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-4y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,4,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-4y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨x-4y+1,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(x-4y+1-4(x+1)+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(-3x-3y-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy+x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2y+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3[{y}^{2}+4y]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =-96\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2675eb0f-c25d-53b2-81e3-8fb563f36009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321447} {:answer/id #uuid "78932147-1c07-5243-a33b-54ccc3b3d246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322095} {:answer/id #uuid "c5320cee-9b68-5526-9f7b-e4e81b3395ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322678} {:answer/id #uuid "d670b8fa-5cf4-557e-9b86-650409c1e001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322820} {:answer/id #uuid "db414795-e6be-52ec-a2e8-11da08ea2311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db414795-e6be-52ec-a2e8-11da08ea2311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322865} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f36e3b8-2489-5327-a723-3c371eeae8a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7134c822-9a7e-5b0a-ae0e-9d8056e17711" :question/math-academy-id "q-365879" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}4x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe33c2d-28d8-5c63-af57-2cfea02124a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370855} {:answer/id #uuid "b144a61b-6449-5a95-93b2-5ecb54d3aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370856} {:answer/id #uuid "c39ce534-8261-5bb2-a3a7-9565f1b35ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370857} {:answer/id #uuid "f6599dfa-b736-51b0-a238-88ebd55fb5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370858} {:answer/id #uuid "da68bb0d-4fa0-572e-849a-ab56c69bc8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe33c2d-28d8-5c63-af57-2cfea02124a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370855} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b73cc72-c77c-588f-8710-d900af9a9255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c5b1199-265f-51a1-b6b8-07c045672fdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-365884" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
